\f1\fs22 Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning\'92s weekday commute was a study in atmospheric irony. Her apartment, perched just above the Layer, was supposedly an Airborne perk, but on days like today the wind licked up dirty tufts of chemical cloud and wafted them at her barren balcony\'97always sunless, anyway, because the neighbouring towers and a thicket of new hover-copt pads cast everything in perpetual gloom.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Resigned, Hayley took the lift up and walked the web of pedestrian bridges to the Rail as last night\'92s drizzle evaporated against acid-mottled plexiglass. Rail, not hover-copt: a detective\'92s salary wouldn\'92t cover private air unless she moonlighted as a gangland widow. She queued for a carriage and was promptly betrayed by her own tech. Her tablet bleated a trilogy of warnings: she\'92d exceeded her monthly transport budget, wasn\'92t logging enough steps to avoid dying seven years early, and was at risk of losing this month\'92s bonus savings rate unless she took advantage of a \'93limited-time\'94 breakfast burrito at the next station.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She dutifully got off, bought the burrito. It tasted like walrus shank preserved in rancid cat fat so she tossed it off the next bridge. The Layer would dissolve every grisly ligament before it hit the sidewalk, and Hayley reasoned this made her a net asset for municipal hygiene.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The last leg of her journey was a bus-sized elevator down to TRAMP Central, a lift so slow that, by law, passengers could claim compensation if they grew a beard between floors. She checked her reflection and, as usual, saw only a hint of exhaustion and what might one day be called wisdom, if she managed to survive another year on the job.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley sat in the windowless investigators\'92 pen, surrounded by walls the colour of cinnamon latte or crushed cockroaches, depending on the light.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Outside, cleaners in hazmat suits scoured the Layer\'92s acidic residue off the building\'92s fa\'e7ades. Not every structure deserved such care; many stood like rotting teeth, gold-crowned for Airborne, with gaping cavities where the Layer chewed deepest. The truly abject Cloudborne lived in these eaten-out ruins\'97an underclass\'92s underclass, hidden from above and below.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s analytic trance broke when a detective slammed his heel into a wall, crushing a moderately large cockroach. Another roach fled upward. The detective hurled the dregs of his coffee at it. Hayley was grateful the season was cool; it would be months before those buggers would fly again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Detective Superintendent?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley shuddered. Viscerally, she knew that voice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander sat perched at her aisle-end. \'93May I sit down?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No,\'94 Hayley said, eyes still on her screen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Unruffled, Alexander checked his notes, then launched into his monologue. \'93I\'92d like to talk to you about the Scarlett Shower Surgeon case.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley huffed and gave him a look. All she saw was a talking head, empty as a balloon. Behind her eyes, she imagined a clown car\'97one leering, round-eared Shakti after another tumbling out and inflating. \'93So you\'92re taking my case.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Alexander looked theatrically wounded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I suppose you want all my files.\'94 Hayley picked up a stack and dropped it, just for drama. She could have given secure digital access, but drama mattered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander wrinkled his nose. \'93I haven\'92t the time for backstory. Just give me the script, and I\'92ll take it from there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The script. You know\'85\'94 He raised his eyebrows, puckered, furrowed, tugged his earlobes\'97a mime\'92s attempt to coax plot out of thin air. It rarely worked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley tried to trance herself away. Maybe, when she came back, he\'92d be gone.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Perhaps the treatment then\'85\'94 Alexander tried, faltering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley snapped, \'93What the tax are you talking about, Shakti? Treatment?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander chortled, delighted to explain. \'93A treatment: the story for your case before you write the whole script. Presumably you already have an MC?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Main culprit.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley pressed her palms down, managing civility. \'93I don\'92t have time for this.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander nodded sympathetically, all to-camera empathy. \'93Of course. I understand. Your station: more cases than you can possibly handle.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley glared.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A new idea flickered in Alexander\'92s expression. \'93Perhaps I might speak to your producer then.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My superior officer?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, no producer is my superior\'97I\'92m executive producer wherever I land\'97but if that\'92s how it\'92s done in Minor Crimes\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley bristled. \'93There\'92s a serial killer on the loose. That\'92s hardly minor.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s smug smile thinned. \'93Of course, you see petty crime. But you\'92re missing the most heinous part of this spree.\'94 Hayley gave nothing; Alexander filled the silence. \'93An Airborne has been kidnapped! A beautiful one\'97that always raises the stakes.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He shuffled in his seat, blushing. Hayley saw the tell: pupils blown wide, ears colouring. \'93Tell me, 
\f2\i Lieutenant
\f1\i0 ,\'94 Hayley intoned, squeezing every ounce of derision into his rank. She remembered him behind the Commissioner at the CEO's press conference, feeding lines.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander straightened with the pride of a kid called on in assembly. \'93What do you want to know, Detective Superintendent?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do you really like the cleaner as the mastermind for everything?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Anthony Chantara.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley caught the glint in his tone. \'93You know this guy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, no,\'94 Alexander lied, poorly. \'93He\'92s done some crime scene cleanup, that\'92s all.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So not a security risk\'97until now?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He knows where Sarah Jacobson is.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know this?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tapped his nose. \'93I\'92ve seen evidence\'97regrettably erased for national security\'97placing Sarah Jacobson with Anthony Chantara after the Stickett Inn bombing. PAPER agents at the Memphis Mafia Bar saw her with Chantara and an associate. John Stoat.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley considered. \'93Perhaps Sarah Jacobson is the ringleader.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander shot out of his seat and menaced. Hayley watched him, doing nothing. Silence: her best weapon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Finally, Alexander switched tactics. \'93What is with you? I\'92d heard you were good. But if this is your best, I\'92m disappointed.\'94 He braced for apologies, for pleas. None came.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, okay then,\'94 Hayley said, turning back to her screen. \'93Good day, Lieutenant.\'94 Lunatic.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander slumped, defeated, gesturing in the air. \'93I\'92m trying to figure you out, but I can\'92t see your role in this. What\'92s your motivation? Character arc? Seriously\'97is there even an origin story? How can anyone make a show out of this if 
\f2\i you
\f1\i0  don\'92t even have wounds or agency? There\'92s no redemption arc. What is your struggle?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley sighed. \'93I\'92m struggling to catch a serial killer.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s where you\'92re wrong. You\'92re trying to 
\f2\i increase your influence
\f1\i0  by catching a serial killer. Look: public safety\'92s great. But popularity, that\'92s the real prize.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The real prize,\'94 she mocked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There, you see? Quick learner.\'94 Alexander grinned. \'93Look, the highest priority is finding Sarah Jacobson, and you can assist on the Scarlett Shower Surgeon subplot\'97lead your production team, keep your best gags. With me executive producer, we\'92ll have this whole show a hit in a year.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This isn\'92t a broadcast, Lieutenant.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All of life is a show. The only difference is production values.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t want mine broadcast.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander laughed. \'93Which is exactly why I\'92m the help you need.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t need help. Or a show.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander shrugged. \'93Is a police officer really a police officer if they don\'92t have a show?\'94
